== E.1 Complete Solutions

#sol-item(1, "D")[
  Co-interior (allied) angles are supplementary: $180 - 71 = 109^degree$. *Chapter 1.*
]

#sol-item(2, "A")[
  The exterior angle equals the sum of the two interior opposite angles: $56 + 35 = 91^degree$. *Chapter 2.*
]

#sol-item(3, "B")[
  The radius meets the tangent at $90^degree$, so in $triangle O T P$: $angle O P T = 180 - 90 - 50 = 40^degree$. *Chapter 3.*
]

#sol-item(4, "C")[
  Two circles of radii $8$ and $3$ with centres $2$ apart: the number of intersections is $0$ (compare $2$ with $11$ and $5$). *Chapter 4.*
]

#sol-item(5, "A")[
  $A = 1/2 d_1 d_2 = 1/2 times 9 times 14 = 63$ cm². *Chapter 5.*
]

#sol-item(6, "D")[
  $V = 1/3 times$ base area $times h = 1/3 times 16 times 9 = 48$ cm³. *Chapter 6.*
]

#sol-item(7, "C")[
  Every meridian is a great circle through the North and South poles; its length is the full circumference of the sphere. *Chapter 7.*
]

#sol-item(8, "B")[
  Perpendicular gradients satisfy $m_1 m_2 = -1$, so $m_2 = -1 -: -2 = frac(1, 2)$. *Chapter 8.*
]

#sol-item(9, "D")[
  $tan theta = frac(9, 40)$ from the definitions SOH CAH TOA. *Chapter 9.*
]

#sol-item(10, "A")[
  $tan 45^degree = h/12$ and $tan 45^degree = 1$, so $h = 12$ m. *Chapter 10.*
]

#sol-item(11, "C")[
  Alternate angles are equal, so the alternate angle is also $149^degree$. *Chapter 1.*
]

#sol-item(12, "B")[
  The exterior angle equals the sum of the two interior opposite angles: $62 + 50 = 112^degree$. *Chapter 2.*
]

#sol-item(13, "D")[
  The angle between the tangents and the angle at the centre are supplementary: $180 - 112 = 68^degree$. *Chapter 3.*
]

#sol-item(14, "B")[
  Two circles of radii $8$ and $8$ with centres $3$ apart: the number of intersections is $2$ (compare $3$ with $16$ and $0$). *Chapter 4.*
]

#sol-item(15, "A")[
  $A = 1/2 d_1 d_2 = 1/2 times 19 times 6 = 57$ cm². *Chapter 5.*
]

#sol-item(16, "C")[
  $V = 1/3 times$ base area $times h = 1/3 times 16 times 12 = 64$ cm³. *Chapter 6.*
]

#sol-item(17, "B")[
  On the equator the radius is $R$, and $1^degree = 110$ km, so the distance is $30 times 110 = 3300$ km. *Chapter 7.*
]

#sol-item(18, "C")[
  $d = sqrt(8^2 + 15^2) = sqrt(289) = 17$. *Chapter 8.*
]

#sol-item(19, "A")[
  $cos 30^degree = sqrt(3)/2$. *Chapter 9.*
]

#sol-item(20, "D")[
  $"070"^degree$ $+ 90^degree = 160^degree$, so the new bearing is $"160"^degree$. *Chapter 10.*
]

#sol-item(21, "D")[
  Angles at a point total $360^degree$: $360 - 97 - 71 - 100 = 92^degree$. *Chapter 1.*
]

#sol-item(22, "A")[
  The base angles are equal and total $180 - 36 = 144^degree$, so each is $72^degree$. *Chapter 2.*
]

#sol-item(23, "B")[
  Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral are supplementary: $180 - 109 = 71^degree$. *Chapter 3.*
]

#sol-item(24, "C")[
  Two circles of radii $8$ and $5$ with centres $6$ apart: the number of intersections is $2$ (compare $6$ with $13$ and $3$). *Chapter 4.*
]

#sol-item(25, "A")[
  Arc $= theta/360 times 2 pi r = 90/360 times 2 times 22/7 times 21 = 33$ cm. *Chapter 5.*
]

